the two decisions to lift currency  restrictions and scrap export taxes will  expose Latin America's third largest  economy to international market forces  in ways not seen for a dozen years  former President Cristina Fernandez  kirschner restricted foreign currency  purchases in 2011 to shore up dwindling  foreign currency reserves most  Argentines who wanted to buy dollars  were limited to a few hundred dollars a  month when US currency was available in  banks at all now that's been changed  those who want to export will be able to  export without asking for permission  those who want to import can import  without permission those who want to buy  dollars can buy dollars those who want  to sell dollars no one is going to go  after them this is how a normal economy  functions in any part of the world over  the last year while the official  exchange rate was fixed around nine  argentine pesos to the dollar a dollar  fetched as much as 16 pesos on the black  market businesses especially those  needing to deal in dollars were deeply  affected while the change will be  celebrated by international investors  and welcomed by many Argentines many  fear a sharp devaluation of the peso and  what that might do to already soaring  prices
 

